{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And he sent, etc. — them, and et Pinchas, etc.</b> The verse's saying <b>and he sent</b> intends to make them emissaries of a mitzvah to take vengeance for HaShem, and emissaries of a mitzvah suffer no harm. And it doubled to say otam a second time, to hint that Pinchas was equal to all of them." "And the reason he chose Pinchas and not Yehoshua and the like — this too is from the wisdom of vengeance, for this is the one who was zealous and wrought wondrous judgments upon them, and he is endowed with wondrous power to destroy the entire impure nation. It is also possible that Moshe was concerned about the prosecution of the people — why they did not protest against those who strayed with harlots, even though they are clean; therefore he acted wisely and sent the one who was zealous with the zeal of HaShem at the risk of his life. Or perhaps this is what the verse precisely means by saying <b>otam and et Pinchas</b> — to say that this one completes what is lacking in that one. Or perhaps this matter was hidden in the heart of the mysteries of wisdom, that the vengeance of Midyan needed to be carried out through Pinchas; and the Master intended it for good, so as not to embarrass Moshe by telling him explicitly, 'Send Pinchas' — for that would indicate that he, Heaven forbid, was disqualified from the deed. Therefore He acted wisely and suspended the matter in Moshe's hand, saying to him, ״Avenge,״ etc., and Moshe on his own knew the right thing to do and sent Pinchas; and through this the counsel of HaShem was accomplished for good, and Moshe, the beloved of HaShem, was not shamed."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:16:03.314886034Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}